WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.086 --> 00:00:05.661
Somehow I managed to knock the gunman to the floor again, and Holmes escaped his clutches.

2
00:00:05.662 --> 00:00:11.211
The stranger squirmed mightily, but I held onto him while Holmes blew a whistle out the window.

3
00:00:11.212 --> 00:00:16.401
In a moment two policemen and Inspector Lestrade burst into the house and came to our rescue.

4
00:00:16.402 --> 00:00:21.640
Holmes and his old friend exchanged greetings, while the two policemen pulled their prisoner upright.

5
00:00:21.641 --> 00:00:27.131
"You fiend!" he shouted at Holmes. "You clever, clever fiend!"

6
00:00:27.132 --> 00:00:31.207
Holmes picked up one of the policemen's lanterns and illuminated the man's face.

7
00:00:31.208 --> 00:00:38.828
"Gentlemen," Holmes said, "may I introduce Colonel Sebastian Moran, one of the best shots that our army has ever produced."

8
00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:45.178
The fierce old man said nothing, but glared at Holmes with a strange mixture of hatred and amazement.

9
00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.442
Holmes calmly looked him up and down.

10
00:00:48.443 --> 00:00:54.380
"Colonel, I haven't had the pleasure of seeing you since you were paying such close attention to me at the Reichenbach Falls."

11
00:00:54.381 --> 00:00:59.732
Snarling with rage, Moran sprang forward, but the policemen kept him from attacking Holmes.

12
00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:05.002
"I never imagined that you'd make use of this conveniently empty house," Holmes went on.

13
00:01:05.003 --> 00:01:09.677
"I thought you'd attack me from the street, where Inspector Lestrade and his men were waiting.

14
00:01:09.678 --> 00:01:12.992
Aside from that, everything has gone as I expected."

15
00:01:12.993 --> 00:01:17.861
Colonel Moran turned to Lestrade. "There's no reason I have to listen to this man.

16
00:01:17.862 --> 00:01:20.678
If you're going to arrest me, then take me away."

17
00:01:20.679 --> 00:01:25.014
"Oh, don't worry, we're going to arrest you . . ." Lestrade paused dramatically.

18
00:01:25.015 --> 00:01:28.666
"For the attempted murder of Mr. Sherlock Holmes."

19
00:01:29.308 --> 00:01:34.286
"No, no, that's not the right charge!" Holmes was busy examining Moran's gun.

20
00:01:34.287 --> 00:01:37.629
"Lestrade, I don't think you realize who you're arresting.

21
00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:41.336
This is the man the whole London police force has been searching for.

22
00:01:41.337 --> 00:01:45.083
You need to charge Colonel Moran with the murder of Ronald Adair."

23
00:01:45.084 --> 00:01:48.877
Lestrade drew in a sharp breath and stared at the man before us.

24
00:01:48.878 --> 00:01:51.083
"Ronald Adair's killer!

25
00:01:51.084 --> 00:01:53.939
But how did he manage that on a street full of people?"

26
00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:59.549
Holmes explained that Moran had used an air gun to shoot Adair through the open window of his study.

27
00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:03.668
"I've been aware of air guns for years, but never actually encountered one."

28
00:02:03.669 --> 00:02:06.026
Holmes handed the weapon to Lestrade.

29
00:02:06.027 --> 00:02:10.669
"They're tremendously powerful yet relatively quiet when compared to other guns."

30
00:02:11.352 --> 00:02:16.478
"So that's how the colonel was able to fire the gun without anyone noticing," Lestrade said.

31
00:02:16.479 --> 00:02:24.940
Holmes nodded. "And now, Watson, if you can endure the draft from a broken window, let's retire to my rooms to talk things over."

32
00:02:24.941 --> 00:02:28.916
*** I was delighted to see that Holmes' rooms were just as I remembered

33
00:02:28.917 --> 00:02:37.279
them. There were two occupants—Mrs. Hudson, who beamed at us both, and the wax dummy, which had played such an important role in the evening's

34
00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.749
adventure. It stood on a small table with one of Holmes' old dressing gowns draped around

35
00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:46.420
it. "Mrs. Hudson, I hope you followed my instructions," Holmes said to his

36
00:02:46.421 --> 00:02:48.675
landlady. "Yes," she

37
00:02:48.676 --> 00:02:54.459
replied. "I always approached the dummy on my knees, so that my shadow wouldn't appear on the

38
00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:59.314
blind." "Excellent!" Holmes rubbed his hands together with

39
00:02:59.315 --> 00:03:01.342
satisfaction. "Did you see where the bullet

40
00:03:01.343 --> 00:03:07.689
went?" Mrs. Hudson handed him the bullet, which had passed through the dummy, bounced off a wall, and landed on the

41
00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:13.093
rug. Holmes threw off his shabby coat and put on his dressing gown, which he took from the

42
00:03:13.094 --> 00:03:17.504
dummy. "And now, Watson, sit in your chair, and we'll discuss this case."

43
00:03:17.505 --> 00:03:22.737
Holmes beckoned to the seat where I'd spent many happy hours, and Mrs. Hudson left to make tea.

44
00:03:22.738 --> 00:03:26.703
"You'll be surprised to learn that Colonel Moran had a distinguished career in the army."

45
00:03:26.704 --> 00:03:30.507
Holmes had no idea what had motivated Moran's turn to crime.

46
00:03:30.507 --> 00:03:36.820
But by the time he had retired from the army and arrived in London, Professor Moriarty was aware of his talents.

47
00:03:37.749 --> 00:03:41.955
Eventually Moran became the criminal mastermind's chief of staff.

48
00:03:41.956 --> 00:03:49.070
Professor Moriarty kept the colonel supplied with money and only used him for high-stakes jobs that an ordinary criminal couldn't handle.

49
00:03:49.071 --> 00:03:54.218
When Moriarty's gang was broken up, there was no evidence to connect Moran with the professor.

50
00:03:54.219 --> 00:03:59.531
"Do you remember when I visited you, and I closed the shutters for fear of an air gun?" Holmes asked.

51
00:03:59.532 --> 00:04:02.713
I admitted that I thought it was rather strange at the time.

52
00:04:02.714 --> 00:04:08.369
"I knew that if anyone had an air gun, it would be Colonel Moran, one of the best shots in the world.

53
00:04:08.369 --> 00:04:14.762
When we were in Switzerland, he followed us with Moriarty, and he's undoubtedly the man who threw rocks at me."

54
00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:19.284
As long as the colonel was free, Holmes would be in danger.

55
00:04:19.285 --> 00:04:23.793
But Holmes couldn't kill Moran because then he would be on trial for murder.

56
00:04:23.794 --> 00:04:29.179
"All I could do was read the crime reports in the newspapers and hope that he'd reveal himself."

57
00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:32.087
"And then came the murder of Ronald Adair . . ."

58
00:04:32.088 --> 00:04:40.810
Holmes nodded. "It sounded like Colonel Moran's type of crime: a wealthy member of society mysteriously killed by what I suspected was an air gun."

59
00:04:40.811 --> 00:04:46.396
When Holmes returned to London, he was instantly spotted by Moran's lookout, who alerted the colonel.

60
00:04:46.397 --> 00:04:52.079
"Colonel Moran couldn't fail to connect my sudden return with his crime." Holmes laughed.

61
00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.799
"He must have been terribly alarmed."

62
00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.613
Holmes knew that Moran would immediately try to kill him, probably with an air gun.

63
00:05:00.421 --> 00:05:04.000
So he placed the bust in the window and alerted the police.

64
00:05:04.001 --> 00:05:08.616
"Those were the men you so cleverly spotted in the doorway," Holmes said to me.

65
00:05:08.617 --> 00:05:11.914
"I can understand why Moran was after you," I said.

66
00:05:11.915 --> 00:05:14.915
"But what was his motive for murdering Adair?"

67
00:05:14.916 --> 00:05:20.527
Holmes explained that Moran and Adair had recently won a lot of money playing whist together.

68
00:05:20.528 --> 00:05:24.301
On the day of the murder, Adair discovered that Moran had been cheating.

69
00:05:24.302 --> 00:05:32.179
Adair probably threatened to reveal what Moran had done unless he immediately resigned from his card clubs and promised never to play cards again.

70
00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:36.361
"But exclusion from his clubs would mean financial ruin," Holmes said.

71
00:05:36.362 --> 00:05:40.634
"Since Moriarty's death, he'd had to find another source of income."

72
00:05:41.327 --> 00:05:45.654
"And he lived on the money he made by winning at cards!" I said.

73
00:05:45.655 --> 00:05:47.523
"So he murdered Adair."

74
00:05:47.524 --> 00:05:53.189
"Exactly," Holmes said. "Adair didn't want to profit from his whist partner's cheating.

75
00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:56.954
That night he was figuring out what he owed his fellow card players.

76
00:05:56.955 --> 00:06:03.198
Adair had locked the door because he didn't want his mother or sister to come in and ask what he was doing with all that money.

77
00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:07.926
Meanwhile Moran had found another convenient hiding place and fired his gun."

78
00:06:07.927 --> 00:06:13.387
"Well, Holmes, once again you've solved the case," I said as Mrs. Hudson brought us some tea.

79
00:06:13.388 --> 00:06:19.804
"And may I say, on behalf of the entire city of London, that we are all delighted to have you back!"

